00:01:25.576 --> 00:01:27.450
And then with two of
our partners, Cloudflare

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.010
and Palo Alto
Networks, how they're

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:31.080
integrating with Cloud
Security Command Center

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.060
and providing
additional visibility

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.950
into customers' signals,
networking, network traffic,

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.970
DOS, and WAF.

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:42.770
And then I'll summarize.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:45.560
So, with the Cloud
Security Command Center,

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:49.640
our goal really is to provide
that canonical place in GCP

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.410
for you to bring all
the things together

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:54.920
that you care about when you
think about security, risk,

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:55.940
and privacy.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:59.210
So it's really where we're
bringing together your assets

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:02.690
from an inventory standpoint,
the detections that you have,

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:06.200
the findings, the policies
that you're running,

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.479
the underlying
annotations that you

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:10.800
want to put specially
on the resources,

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.240
bring that together so you
have a unified view of that,

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.710
whether those signals
come from Google products,

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:19.280
from third-party products,
from your own detectors.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.100
And our goal really is to kind
of give back to you visibility

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.050
and control so that
you have the ability

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:26.204
to look at your attack surface.

00:02:26.204 --> 00:02:28.370
So some of the key things
that, from a functionality

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:31.340
standpoint, the purpose of the
Cloud Security Command Center

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:33.170
is really to gather
and integrate

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:35.970
all of that information
at an organization level

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.430
so you get to look across
different projects and services

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.130
as you're running.

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:42.430
To allow you, as we said,
to understand and analyze

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:45.860
that information, to
act on that information,

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:48.950
and really to be a hub for
a range of Google security

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:51.352
products all in one place.

00:02:51.352 --> 00:02:53.810
And we recognize that we're
not the only player that you're

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:56.640
going to have, especially if
you're in a hybrid situation,

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.170
so our goal has been to design
this product and this platform

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:03.920
as API first so you can
as well egress and export

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.980
this information to your own
systems for further analysis.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:12.040
When we think about the general
kind of attacker or defender

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:14.250
kill chain-- and we
see here the attacker

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:17.560
kill chain popularized by
Lockheed Martin in 2011.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:20.789
At Google, we also think
about a defender kill chain,

00:03:20.789 --> 00:03:22.330
and for the defender
kill chain side,

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:24.550
we think about two
phases of that--

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:27.356
being proactive
in a pre-fail mode

00:03:27.356 --> 00:03:29.230
so that you have an
assessment of really what

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:31.880
your overall attack surface
is, what you care about,

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:35.020
what you should be focused
on, and what matters to you.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:36.550
To really be able
to specify ahead

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:39.520
of time what are the policies,
what are the configurations

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.590
that you want to
run, and also to be

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:44.620
able to then monitor the
drift against those and also

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:46.090
deploy protections--

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:48.460
things that will
by design enable

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:50.140
you to kind of
prevent certain types

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:52.120
of attacks and vulnerabilities.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.790
We also then think about
what happens in post-fail--

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:57.310
when monitoring and logging
turn into detection.

00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:59.659
So the key signals that
you find are the ones

00:03:59.659 --> 00:04:01.450
that you're going to
operate on when you're

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:03.214
investigating an incident.

00:04:03.214 --> 00:04:04.630
Obviously alerting
on those things

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:07.420
so that your teams-- your
incident response teams

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:10.540
can act on those and your
SOC can act on those.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:12.950
To be able to
actually then respond,

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:16.450
whether it's in a full
fix or a mitigation.

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:19.600
To then be able to manage your
communications across those.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.579
And also, very
importantly, to learn--

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:23.616
and that's learn from
a process standpoint,

00:04:23.616 --> 00:04:25.990
from a people standpoint, as
well from a machine learning

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:27.860
technology standpoint.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:30.700
The Cloud Security
Command Center in v1

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.340
is focused on the first
half of this chain--

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:35.650
being able to enable you
to do a much better job

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:37.600
of proactive protection
by giving you

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:41.380
the visibility on those
assets and those findings.

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:44.290
When we think about how
this plays in context,

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:46.210
this is a framework
that we look at when

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:49.910
we think about helping our
customers become more secure.

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:52.570
So at a high level, we
think about the risks

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:55.810
that you're looking at
really in five categories.

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.360
They're really securing
the underlying inventory--

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:00.520
and that's the inventory
of hardware and software

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:02.410
you're running in your systems--

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:05.470
securing the data-- that's
data that either you've created

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:08.890
or data that you've captured
from your end customers--

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:11.680
securing the
configurations so that each

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.250
of the services you start off
in is in a best practice known

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:18.280
good state and understanding
the drift against that,

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:21.340
securing your identity
and management design

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:23.740
so that you have the proper
amount of access and control

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:25.870
to the different
permissions and policies,

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:28.540
and then helping you secure
the applications that you build

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:30.460
on our own infrastructure.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:32.071
So matching that
are the capabilities

00:05:32.071 --> 00:05:34.570
we're building into the Cloud
Security Command Center, which

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:37.100
we'll demo in a few minutes.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:39.250
First is the asset
inventory to give you

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:41.470
that view of both
the current state

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:44.540
and historical state of assets.

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:47.500
The ability to track when
the assets have changed

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:49.570
and how they've been
modified and to really

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:52.870
manage those assets and group
them in business-relevant ways

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:55.450
beyond just what's in
the resource hierarchy.

00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:56.920
In addition, one
of the core things

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:59.530
and what will be exciting
to hear from both Two Sigma,

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:02.680
Cloudflare, and Palo Alto,
is bringing in signals

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:06.730
in to findings that matter
to customers in one place

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:08.900
so they can make
decisions on that.

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:12.170
And then the ability really
to provide you as a customer

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:14.110
the ability to create
filters and queries

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:16.600
to find the specific
data that you want.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:19.870
Obviously the visibility side
is one part and then the action

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:22.810
part is the other, so the
ability to mark things so that

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:25.240
you can group them by
priority, the ability to create

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:28.690
notifications through a Sidecar
application that we ship with

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:32.320
the product, the ability as well
to do mitigation remediations--

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:35.980
again through a Sidecar
connection to cloud functions--

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:38.230
and then finally, to be
that hub for the security

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:41.170
products like our Anomaly
Detector, our Web Security

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:45.010
Scanner, and our DOP technology.

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:47.820
So what you'll see in
the demo forthcoming

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:50.670
is really the UI part
of the Command Center.

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:54.000
That UI really has
three main surfaces.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.370
The first is the
Dashboard, which is really

00:06:56.370 --> 00:07:00.360
the overall view of what's
going on in your system

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:02.550
and gives you an ability
to understand and look

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:05.850
at what's hot, what's happening,
what you should focus on.

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:08.370
And then where we see
analysts and security

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:10.080
professionals spend
most of their time

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:11.610
is in the inventories.

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:13.260
We have an Asset
Inventory so that you

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.420
can take an asset-centric
view of what's

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:17.760
going on in your system,
as well as a Findings

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:20.250
Inventory that you can take
a finding-centric view,

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:22.720
and they both
cross-leverage each other.

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:24.870
And then finally, for
any entity that we've

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:28.259
indexed, whether it's an asset,
a resource, or a finding,

00:07:28.259 --> 00:07:30.300
we have the ability to
drill in at a detail level

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:33.490
so you can look like the
underlying properties.

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:34.890
So without further
ado, I'm going

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:36.014
to switch over to the demo.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:41.710
So what you're seeing here
now is the live version

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:43.600
of the Cloud Security
Command Center.

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:46.840
It basically is an organization
that we've created.

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:50.220
That organization has
a bunch of assets--

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:54.010
routes, subnetworks, firewalls,
buckets, instances, disks.

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:56.110
You can look at it
historically, so we can see

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:58.330
what changed seven days ago.

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:01.450
So over the last seven days,
we've added a bunch of routes,

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:03.634
we've deleted one of
the unknown things,

00:08:03.634 --> 00:08:05.800
we've looked at the firewalls,
we've gone and looked

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:07.420
at the overall disk pieces.

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:09.850
We go back, say, three
months, and you'll

00:08:09.850 --> 00:08:12.730
see that the system has
even changed more radically

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:15.200
over that period of time.

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:17.800
And this gives you kind of
an ongoing record time series

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:20.030
of how your assets data is.

00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:22.270
In addition, we have
the findings side

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:25.090
of the world, which is looking
at the various signals that

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:28.570
are integrated in to tell
you what we've collected

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:32.799
around either good, bad findings
for each of the underlying

00:08:32.799 --> 00:08:33.919
assets.

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:36.519
So first is an overall
findings summary card,

00:08:36.519 --> 00:08:40.120
which takes a look at what
are the different detectors

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:43.090
and what have they surfaced,
and then we start with three

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:44.500
of the Google detectors.

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:46.510
So we have a Cloud
Anomaly Detector

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:49.390
that allows you on a
VM basis to understand

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:52.540
when we think there is a
hijacking going on, when there

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:56.230
potentially coin mining,
data exfiltration,

00:08:56.230 --> 00:08:58.210
potentially if your VM
has been compromised

00:08:58.210 --> 00:08:59.980
and being used in a DOS net.

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:02.740
If your VM has been compromised
and it's being used to try to

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:05.290
intrude on other systems.

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:08.320
In addition, we have our
Web Security Scanner which

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:10.780
is available in
GA and App Engine

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:13.799
and is in Public
Alpha for GCE and GKE,

00:09:13.799 --> 00:09:16.090
and that lets you find things
like cross-site scripting

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:21.430
vulnerabilities, outdated
libraries, mixed content.

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:24.880
You can run that on your
publicly-facing applications

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:27.580
and those results automatically
surface in the Command Center

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:29.170
when there are findings.

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:31.930
In addition, we're integrated
with the DLP technology

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:35.680
that we announced, I think,
more than a year ago at Next,

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:39.610
which allows you to scan
all of your public buckets

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:42.670
and scan all your buckets in
your system and then classifies

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:45.550
whether there is PII
found in those buckets.

00:09:45.550 --> 00:09:47.470
And that gives you a
sense of the surface area

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:50.320
and the blast radius of
when things potentially

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:52.300
are compromised,
where your data is,

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:55.150
what data is highly sensitive.

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:57.730
In addition, we're
integrated with Forseti.

00:09:57.730 --> 00:10:00.460
So Forseti is Google's
open-source effort

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:03.250
to build a security
community around GCP

00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:07.540
and has a set of scanners that
both Google has contributed

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:10.960
as well as our partners
and community members

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:14.680
and Forseti have contributed,
which those results also index

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:17.980
and can be surfaced
in Command Center.

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:21.040
And then we have a range
of partner integrations.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:24.470
From the container security
side, Cloudflare, which Kamilla

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:26.770
will talk about in more detail.

00:10:26.770 --> 00:10:30.220
Folks like CrowdStrike,
Dome9, Palo Alto Networks,

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:32.950
which Francesco will talk
about in more detail.

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:36.389
Qualys, RedLock, Sysdig,
StackRox, and Twistlock.

00:10:36.389 --> 00:10:38.680
So that gives you a sense
of-- and this would obviously

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:40.900
be a really unhappy system
if all of these bad thigs

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:43.140
were happening to it.

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:45.840
So, as I said,
this is that place

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:49.004
where customers go on a daily
basis to see what's going on,

00:10:49.004 --> 00:10:50.670
and then if they want
to go investigate,

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:53.610
they would dig into something
like the Asset Inventory.

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:55.800
So the structure of
the Asset Inventory

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:57.480
is one that, first,
you can slice

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:00.120
your data based on
either the projects

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:02.490
or the underlying assets.

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:09.030
And as we said, we cover App
Engine, GCE, GKE, GCR docker

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:12.630
images, and then GCS from a
bucket standpoint currently,

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:16.552
and we'll be continuously adding
more of the Google services.

00:11:16.552 --> 00:11:18.510
You can take that view
of either-- for example,

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:19.920
I want to look at
all my buckets.

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:21.720
You can just truncate on that.

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:24.980
Or instead, if I wanted to
go to a particular project--

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:28.920
and let's say I want to
go to this dev project

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:32.970
and then I wanted to look
at just all the buckets that

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:36.780
are in that, so
I would basically

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:40.084
select Attribute Asset
Type, type Buckets,

00:11:40.084 --> 00:11:42.000
and then this filters
down to just the buckets

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:43.206
within that project.

00:11:43.206 --> 00:11:44.580
So the way we've
constructed this

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:47.460
is what you select on the left
in the kind of overall resource

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:48.300
hierarchy.

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:50.070
On the right, you
get only the assets

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:51.960
that belong to that
resource hierarchy,

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:54.639
and you can further
filter things.

00:11:54.639 --> 00:11:56.180
So one of the things
that we can look

00:11:56.180 --> 00:11:58.180
for which people care
about is, you know,

00:11:58.180 --> 00:12:00.740
what if I expose one
of my buckets publicly?

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:02.030
How would I find that?

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:05.750
So first, I would look-- again,
I'd go across my organization.

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:09.030
I'd want to go ahead and
search again for my buckets.

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:14.940
And then once I've
searched for my buckets,

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:18.340
I'd want to look at
one of the properties

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:20.260
and that would be
the ACL property,

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:26.450
and let's say if I have an All
Authenticated Users on that.

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:29.830
And sure enough, we have one
bucket we've exposed to public.

00:12:29.830 --> 00:12:30.430
Right?

00:12:30.430 --> 00:12:32.320
So what does that mean?

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:34.210
We can dig into that
a little bit more.

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:36.610
That bucket basically
has a set of attributes,

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:39.100
a set of detail
properties specific to it.

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:43.000
You can see right here that
the All Authenticated Users has

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:47.856
been set up as a reader, so if
that's intended, that's great,

00:12:47.856 --> 00:12:50.230
but if that's a bucket with
highly sensitive information,

00:12:50.230 --> 00:12:53.080
that would be bad and you'd
want to remediate that.

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:55.210
The other thing that we
want to cover a little bit

00:12:55.210 --> 00:12:57.710
is from a time standpoint.

00:12:57.710 --> 00:13:02.890
So if we go back to this
demo, this changed at about--

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:05.530
let's see, last scan at 3:58.

00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:08.790
So if we were to look
back at yesterday--

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:10.990
and we can go back
to yesterday--

00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:14.440
we can figure out whether
this bucket was actually

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:16.460
public yesterday or not.

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:19.210
So as of yesterday, it
wasn't, so it happened

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:20.510
some time between now and then.

00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:22.180
And we can look
at, then, the logs

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:24.800
to figure out when
that occurred.

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:26.860
In addition, from an
asset inventory side,

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:29.750
we can also slice things
based on findings.

00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:31.590
So from the findings,
first you see

00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:33.170
you have, again, two choices.

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:36.010
You can see things either listed
by all the possible findings

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:39.820
or you can sort them by
the particular source type.

00:13:39.820 --> 00:13:43.194
And so we have findings from
each of the underlying sources.

00:13:43.194 --> 00:13:44.860
If we were to dig
into a finding-- let's

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:48.100
say our own Anomaly
Detector, we could

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:52.570
find and dig into the
VM that has coin mining.

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:55.300
And what that ends up telling
again on our Detail page

00:13:55.300 --> 00:13:58.060
is what action should be
taken against that, what it's

00:13:58.060 --> 00:14:00.610
against, that it's
an active finding,

00:14:00.610 --> 00:14:03.460
and an underlying summary
of what's going on.

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:08.290
For third parties, like
Cloudflare or Palo Alto,

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:09.970
when we have an
integration there,

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:12.820
they surface their
findings as a summary.

00:14:12.820 --> 00:14:16.830
Just like any other finding,
you can drill into that finding.

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:19.000
They provide underlying detail.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:21.910
And then you can connect
back to the partner console.

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:25.970
So go back into the Cloudflare
system, in this example,

00:14:25.970 --> 00:14:28.750
look at that particular
website that's involved,

00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:31.354
and then dig into the
underlying data there as well.

00:14:31.354 --> 00:14:32.770
So that's the kind
of integrations

00:14:32.770 --> 00:14:37.950
that we have with the
underlying providers.

00:14:37.950 --> 00:14:41.752
So to wrap up this
part of the demo,

00:14:41.752 --> 00:14:43.210
and what we've
covered today really

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:45.340
is some of the
underlying capabilities

00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:47.020
for filtering and searching.

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:48.460
One last thing we
have the ability

00:14:48.460 --> 00:14:50.860
to do is for you
to create marks,

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:53.440
and what those marks allow
you to do is to group data

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:54.920
by things that matter.

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:59.970
So for example, I created a mark
previously around an incident

00:14:59.970 --> 00:15:04.810
and I care about all the
different findings that

00:15:04.810 --> 00:15:06.100
are relative to that incident.

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:08.920
So I've already pre-marked them
so that becomes these eight

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:12.790
or nine findings, and
similarly, on the asset side,

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:16.325
I've also put that mark together
on some of the assets that

00:15:16.325 --> 00:15:17.200
are relevant to this.

00:15:24.070 --> 00:15:26.950
And so as we go ahead and then
put the same incident number

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:29.920
in, it pre-filters
down to those,

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:31.970
and then I can go
in the Dashboard

00:15:31.970 --> 00:15:34.300
and again look and see
just for the assets that

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:37.240
are relevant to that incident
as well as the findings that

00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:38.950
are relevant to that incident.

00:15:38.950 --> 00:15:41.300
So with this, I'm going to
switch back to the slides.

00:15:45.460 --> 00:15:48.040
And so what we covered there
was the Asset Inventory,

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:50.410
the Findings, the ability
to filter and search,

00:15:50.410 --> 00:15:52.780
the ability to use the marks,
and also the integrations

00:15:52.780 --> 00:15:56.030
with a range of our partners.

00:15:56.030 --> 00:15:57.980
What we now want
to talk about is,

00:15:57.980 --> 00:16:00.761
as one of the core parts in
the differentiation for Command

00:16:00.761 --> 00:16:03.010
Center is the ability to
integrate all those findings,

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:04.718
so we're going to dig
a little bit deeper

00:16:04.718 --> 00:16:06.620
into what that means,
what findings are,

00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:10.290
what finding sources are.

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:13.410
So for us, we've defined
findings very broadly.

00:16:13.410 --> 00:16:16.770
They really are an
output from a source,

00:16:16.770 --> 00:16:18.180
and it could be
a curated output,

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:22.110
it could be a raw output,
that then applies to an asset

00:16:22.110 --> 00:16:24.480
that we've indexed or a
resource that we've indexed

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:26.310
and it allows us to
build that connection

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:28.980
to do either a finding-centric
view of the world

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:31.010
or an asset-centric
view of the world.

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:33.060
And it's valid for
a particular time

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:35.190
or between two time
points, so that

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:38.730
allows us to tie that into
its history and its lifecycle.

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:41.680
And they need to have a unique
ID because we automatically

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:43.960
dedupe findings within
the Command Center,

00:16:43.960 --> 00:16:46.737
so if you have the same
finding six or seven times,

00:16:46.737 --> 00:16:48.570
it doesn't show up as
six or seven findings.

00:16:48.570 --> 00:16:51.000
It shows up as the same
finding and it just

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:54.580
updates its properties and
its time stamp as it goes.

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:57.240
And so that allows things to
be correctly indexed and joined

00:16:57.240 --> 00:17:00.060
in the Command Center database.

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:02.970
When we think about findings,
they come from finding sources,

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:05.760
and our mental model so
far for finding sources

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:10.800
is really around the ability
to think of three main types--

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:15.270
scanners, which really provide
you essentially a snapshot

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:17.970
in time of the state
of your whole system

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:21.329
or the state between
two time points,

00:17:21.329 --> 00:17:23.730
and what that allows
us to do is to say

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:26.430
that represents the
kind of complete state

00:17:26.430 --> 00:17:28.380
that we want to integrate
in and see what's

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:30.180
going on for that time stamp.

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:33.990
Examples of scanners are the
Cloud Security Scanner that

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:36.840
we've integrated within Google,
the DLP scanner as well--

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:40.590
they both scan the state of
either an application or a set

00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:42.690
of buckets at a point in time--

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:44.545
the standard L3L4
network scanners

00:17:44.545 --> 00:17:46.170
that are scanning
ports, because again,

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:48.295
if they have an overall
scan across a set of ports,

00:17:48.295 --> 00:17:50.740
they have underlying results.

00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:53.460
Things from the container
image scanners from folks

00:17:53.460 --> 00:17:55.320
like Twistlock.

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:57.570
And the second type
of finding source

00:17:57.570 --> 00:17:59.460
that we integrate
with are monitors,

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:01.715
and so those are things
that produce event streams.

00:18:01.715 --> 00:18:03.090
So the common
example of that are

00:18:03.090 --> 00:18:07.650
agents that are put into
either VMs or into containers

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:09.420
and they prevent
basically a stream

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:13.450
of events with no history
back to the system.

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:16.710
And the third type of source
that we can integrate with

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:17.910
are really loggers.

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:20.460
So that's the canonical
log file ingestion,

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:22.710
where that's a series
and historical record

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:25.650
of a series of events that
get adjusted and processed.

00:18:25.650 --> 00:18:29.395
So if you as a customer or you
as a partner have one or more

00:18:29.395 --> 00:18:31.020
of these types, these
are types that we

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:34.410
can integrate in and have
their results join together

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:39.300
with the rest of the findings in
Cloud Security Command Center.

00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:41.880
Think a little bit more
about how that happens.

00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:46.536
What we show here is a
screenshot of our alpha API.

00:18:46.536 --> 00:18:47.660
It's a little hard to read.

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:50.100
There's a link directly to
our documentation, which

00:18:50.100 --> 00:18:53.970
is a public documentation of
how you use the Create Findings

00:18:53.970 --> 00:18:55.140
API.

00:18:55.140 --> 00:18:58.290
The key thing is
that there are really

00:18:58.290 --> 00:19:01.030
seven things that
you need to provide

00:19:01.030 --> 00:19:04.320
as someone who wants to write
results to our Command Center.

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:06.990
The first, as we talked
about, is that unique ID

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:09.180
so that we can do
our own deduping.

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:11.280
The second is the
actual insight that you

00:19:11.280 --> 00:19:14.580
want to surface in Command
Center, both in the dashboard

00:19:14.580 --> 00:19:17.940
and as kind of the key title
in indexing for the Findings

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:19.170
Inventory.

00:19:19.170 --> 00:19:23.880
The third thing is the mapping
to the one or more Google Cloud

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:26.610
assets that these
findings apply to

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:28.500
and the fourth thing
is a time stamp.

00:19:28.500 --> 00:19:30.960
So as we are a
historical record,

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:33.510
we're able to put
that finding in place

00:19:33.510 --> 00:19:37.060
relative to the other
things in our time database.

00:19:37.060 --> 00:19:40.730
And then lastly, the
link back to-- if you're

00:19:40.730 --> 00:19:43.650
a commercial vendor,
your own portal,

00:19:43.650 --> 00:19:46.350
because our goal and our
philosophy with integration

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:50.190
is to really surface the
key things that we think

00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:53.220
are relevant to the customer,
but if they want to go in deep

00:19:53.220 --> 00:19:55.620
and dive in more
detail, we fully

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:58.470
expect that they'd want to
go into your own dashboard

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:01.330
and explore those using the
tools that you've invested in.

00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:04.120
So that linkage, as we showed
with the Cloudflare example,

00:20:04.120 --> 00:20:06.520
provides that two-way
kind of transition.

00:20:06.520 --> 00:20:08.790
And then lastly,
the key thing to add

00:20:08.790 --> 00:20:11.140
is finding specific properties.

00:20:11.140 --> 00:20:15.750
So when you surface a finding on
your side as either a customer

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:19.020
or as a partner,
identifying the key items

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:21.270
that you want the end
customer to see that

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:22.710
will drive them
to an action that

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:26.830
will help them get to
a more secure space.

00:20:26.830 --> 00:20:29.490
What partners get from
integrating with the Cloud

00:20:29.490 --> 00:20:31.240
Security Command Center
and what they gain

00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:34.570
are really showing up
in four key places.

00:20:34.570 --> 00:20:36.670
First, in the Summary
on the Dashboard,

00:20:36.670 --> 00:20:39.400
as we saw with each of
the dashboard cards.

00:20:39.400 --> 00:20:42.310
Second, in the inventories,
both actually in the Findings

00:20:42.310 --> 00:20:45.070
Inventory and in the Asset
Inventory because the assets

00:20:45.070 --> 00:20:48.100
can index into the findings,
into the specific Detail

00:20:48.100 --> 00:20:51.130
pages which allow the
customer to drive action,

00:20:51.130 --> 00:20:53.620
and then actually into
our Datacube Index, which

00:20:53.620 --> 00:20:57.190
is our back-end database which
allows you to search, select,

00:20:57.190 --> 00:20:58.870
and find API and Views.

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:04.940
If we think about integration,
there's really three options

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:07.360
if you're a customer
or a partner of how

00:21:07.360 --> 00:21:10.420
you can integrate data into the
Cloud Security Command Center.

00:21:10.420 --> 00:21:14.290
The first option assumes that
you have a presence in GCP

00:21:14.290 --> 00:21:18.640
itself, and so in this
model, as a customer,

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:20.950
the customer has an
organization in GCP,

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:23.080
you have an organization in GCP.

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:26.830
The first step is for
you to essentially scan

00:21:26.830 --> 00:21:30.090
the customer's instance or
instances or organizations

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:32.480
so that you find
out what's going on.

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:35.590
The second is to spin
up a connector project

00:21:35.590 --> 00:21:38.080
in your own organization,
which will end up

00:21:38.080 --> 00:21:40.780
being the intermediary
between your overall back-end

00:21:40.780 --> 00:21:42.850
and our Cloud Security
Command Center.

00:21:42.850 --> 00:21:46.180
The third is to do then
the authorization so

00:21:46.180 --> 00:21:48.670
that your service
account for your project

00:21:48.670 --> 00:21:50.620
is authorized to write
to the Security Command

00:21:50.620 --> 00:21:52.564
Center for the
customers, as well

00:21:52.564 --> 00:21:53.980
as you do your own
authorizations,

00:21:53.980 --> 00:21:55.870
your own back-end,
to uniquely identify

00:21:55.870 --> 00:21:58.480
the customer's information
that you want to send.

00:21:58.480 --> 00:22:02.015
And then to push the
data from your back-end

00:22:02.015 --> 00:22:04.390
into the Cloud Security Command
Center via that connector

00:22:04.390 --> 00:22:06.230
project.

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:11.290
The second option is if you're
not running on GCP at all

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:15.160
is to instantiate
your connector project

00:22:15.160 --> 00:22:18.670
after you do the scanning in
the customer's organization.

00:22:18.670 --> 00:22:20.440
So to provide the
basic underlying

00:22:20.440 --> 00:22:24.160
code that allows the customer to
stand up that connection point.

00:22:24.160 --> 00:22:27.880
Again, authorization both
towards us and the customer's

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:30.540
Cloud Security Command Center
as well as to your own back-end

00:22:30.540 --> 00:22:32.590
so the right data
gets the right place

00:22:32.590 --> 00:22:36.370
and then connecting the
data through and through.

00:22:36.370 --> 00:22:40.690
And the third option is if you
are not running anything on GCP

00:22:40.690 --> 00:22:45.000
and you have completely partner
back-end is again to first scan

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:49.620
the customer's organization
for the results that you want

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:54.090
to run, and then through
the customer, authenticate--

00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:56.430
allow them to authenticate
into your system--

00:22:56.430 --> 00:22:59.370
provide a nice API
key that gives them

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:02.610
access to the Command Center,
and once that API is installed

00:23:02.610 --> 00:23:05.400
on your back-end, you can then
write to the Security Command

00:23:05.400 --> 00:23:07.589
Center specific to
that organization.

00:23:07.589 --> 00:23:08.880
So those are the three methods.

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:12.030
We've had customers and partners
use each of these three methods

00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:13.290
to do integration.

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:15.490
And if you want to find
out more information,

00:23:15.490 --> 00:23:18.660
please find me after the talk.

00:23:18.660 --> 00:23:22.190
So to summarize the kind
of first part of the Cloud

00:23:22.190 --> 00:23:24.940
Security Command Center
is, at the end of the day,

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:26.630
the goal is to
integrate in one place

00:23:26.630 --> 00:23:30.560
and unify our assets,
resources, and findings.

00:23:30.560 --> 00:23:33.350
If you want to integrate your
findings in the Command Center,

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:37.350
our partner model is really
set up in three stages.

00:23:37.350 --> 00:23:40.940
First, a scenario definition
between the product managers

00:23:40.940 --> 00:23:43.760
so that collectively we're
looking at the end customer's

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:45.650
journey so that the
right set of data

00:23:45.650 --> 00:23:48.500
gets surfaced in Command
Center and the right pull

00:23:48.500 --> 00:23:51.140
and kind of pull through
occurs to drive them back

00:23:51.140 --> 00:23:52.640
to your own console.

00:23:52.640 --> 00:23:55.950
Prototyping so that we build
an example first within a demo

00:23:55.950 --> 00:23:58.880
organization that us
and the partner manage,

00:23:58.880 --> 00:24:01.880
and then find a lead
customer to kind of go deploy

00:24:01.880 --> 00:24:04.430
that live with, and then
finally, the execution

00:24:04.430 --> 00:24:08.120
deployment at scale for general
availability for customers.

00:24:08.120 --> 00:24:09.620
So with that, I've
kind of given you

00:24:09.620 --> 00:24:11.661
an overview of both the
capabilities of the Cloud

00:24:11.661 --> 00:24:13.760
Command Center as
well as how partners

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:15.260
integrate with Command Center.

00:24:15.260 --> 00:24:17.120
What I'd like to do now
is to turn this over

00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:18.809
to Michael Capicotto
from Two Sigma

00:24:18.809 --> 00:24:21.350
to talk to you a little about
how they've used Cloud Security

00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:21.975
Command Center.

00:24:27.437 --> 00:24:29.520
MICHAEL CAPICOTTO: So my
name's Michael Capicotto.

00:24:29.520 --> 00:24:32.190
I'm a cloud security
architect at Two Sigma,

00:24:32.190 --> 00:24:34.210
and I kind of
wanted to introduce

00:24:34.210 --> 00:24:37.710
who Two Sigma is, what we do,
and a little bit about how

00:24:37.710 --> 00:24:39.630
we approach cloud
security before I show you

00:24:39.630 --> 00:24:42.400
what we're doing with the
Security Command Center.

00:24:42.400 --> 00:24:45.750
So Two Sigma is a New York
City-based hedge fund.

00:24:45.750 --> 00:24:49.770
We use things like artificial
intelligence, machine learning,

00:24:49.770 --> 00:24:52.620
distributed computing-- all of
kind of the latest technologies

00:24:52.620 --> 00:24:55.470
to drive our
investment strategy.

00:24:55.470 --> 00:24:58.374
And for that reason,
operating in the public cloud

00:24:58.374 --> 00:24:59.790
has been extremely
important to us

00:24:59.790 --> 00:25:03.690
so that we can scale up and
scale down very dynamically.

00:25:03.690 --> 00:25:06.180
To give you an example,
we use something

00:25:06.180 --> 00:25:10.130
like 35 petabytes of data to
build our investment models,

00:25:10.130 --> 00:25:14.460
and this is a growing
number, and so

00:25:14.460 --> 00:25:17.850
one of the primary concerns
we have in cloud security

00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:20.130
is really protecting
against data exfiltration.

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:22.980
So all of the code and data
that we write and generate,

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:26.560
it's very important for us to
protect against this leaking,

00:25:26.560 --> 00:25:27.060
right?

00:25:27.060 --> 00:25:28.170
That's our job.

00:25:28.170 --> 00:25:30.930
So that's the approach we take.

00:25:30.930 --> 00:25:33.570
And we really think about
it in three ways, right?

00:25:33.570 --> 00:25:36.180
We use defense and depth so
that we have a layered approach

00:25:36.180 --> 00:25:37.050
to security.

00:25:37.050 --> 00:25:39.090
If we kind of make a
mistake in one area,

00:25:39.090 --> 00:25:41.820
we can fall back on other areas.

00:25:41.820 --> 00:25:44.200
We use automation
very heavily, right?

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:46.380
As the public cloud has moved--

00:25:46.380 --> 00:25:48.600
I guess as we've moved
into the public cloud,

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:51.660
we've seen things scale
up a lot quicker, right?

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:55.140
People can procure VMs in
a few seconds as opposed

00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:57.660
to, like, a few months,
and so obviously the attack

00:25:57.660 --> 00:25:59.880
surface scales very
quickly and this means

00:25:59.880 --> 00:26:01.170
that we have to react quickly.

00:26:01.170 --> 00:26:03.780
So we use automation from
a security perspective

00:26:03.780 --> 00:26:07.500
for things like secure approval
and deployment pipelines-- very

00:26:07.500 --> 00:26:09.889
repeatable security processes.

00:26:09.889 --> 00:26:12.180
And then of course there is,
like, the continuous audit

00:26:12.180 --> 00:26:14.430
piece and that's
extremely important to us,

00:26:14.430 --> 00:26:16.680
because even though you
set up your environment

00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:20.010
in a secure manner on day
one, six months later or two

00:26:20.010 --> 00:26:21.900
years later, it's
going to drift, right?

00:26:21.900 --> 00:26:24.760
The configuration and the
environment's going to change,

00:26:24.760 --> 00:26:27.690
and so you want to have
some sort of system that's

00:26:27.690 --> 00:26:30.250
continuously auditing
your environment

00:26:30.250 --> 00:26:32.220
to kind of have control
over what's going on

00:26:32.220 --> 00:26:35.770
and to react to any changes.

00:26:35.770 --> 00:26:39.200
So just to talk a little bit
more about defense in depth,

00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:41.430
if you think all the way
up at a very high level,

00:26:41.430 --> 00:26:44.250
you have your
organization and GCP,

00:26:44.250 --> 00:26:47.877
and you can apply something
like an organization policy.

00:26:47.877 --> 00:26:49.710
What this does, just
to give you an example,

00:26:49.710 --> 00:26:52.560
is it's really like
a blanket security

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:54.330
control on your whole org.

00:26:54.330 --> 00:26:56.850
So one example of how we
use that is we build, like,

00:26:56.850 --> 00:26:59.520
a secure image-- say a
secure Debian image--

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:01.410
with all of the
security controls

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:03.240
and OS hardening
kind of mechanisms

00:27:03.240 --> 00:27:06.180
that we use, and then we
apply an org policy that

00:27:06.180 --> 00:27:10.080
says, in any of the folders or
projects that Two Sigma has,

00:27:10.080 --> 00:27:12.690
we want users to
only be spinning up

00:27:12.690 --> 00:27:14.410
VMs from that secure image.

00:27:14.410 --> 00:27:14.910
Right?

00:27:14.910 --> 00:27:18.330
So that's kind of all the
way at the top of the stack.

00:27:18.330 --> 00:27:20.730
Bubbling down a little bit
into the individual folders

00:27:20.730 --> 00:27:24.300
and projects, we create
custom IM roles for our users

00:27:24.300 --> 00:27:28.031
and groups, and we do this based
on a least permissive model,

00:27:28.031 --> 00:27:28.530
right?

00:27:28.530 --> 00:27:30.690
So only the kind of
minimum set of permissions

00:27:30.690 --> 00:27:33.600
those users need to
operate successfully

00:27:33.600 --> 00:27:34.480
in their environment.

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:37.220
We create a custom role
and assign it to them.

00:27:37.220 --> 00:27:39.960
Things like VPC
Security Controls

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:41.970
to kind of draw
a secure boundary

00:27:41.970 --> 00:27:43.770
around all of our resources.

00:27:43.770 --> 00:27:46.930
And then all the way down to
the individual resource level.

00:27:46.930 --> 00:27:49.440
So if you think about
like BigQuery data sets,

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:52.395
you can have access control
lists on those actual data

00:27:52.395 --> 00:27:53.790
sets.

00:27:53.790 --> 00:27:55.710
Another example is a
GCS bucket, which I'm

00:27:55.710 --> 00:27:57.990
going to be using in the demo.

00:27:57.990 --> 00:28:00.570
So those both have concept of
access control lists and you

00:28:00.570 --> 00:28:04.330
can control the security at
the individual resource level.

00:28:04.330 --> 00:28:04.830
Right?

00:28:04.830 --> 00:28:07.410
And then speaking a little
bit more about the automation

00:28:07.410 --> 00:28:11.670
we use, there are some, I
guess, unexpected consequences

00:28:11.670 --> 00:28:13.770
of using certain GCP APIs.

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:15.810
So to give you an
example of that,

00:28:15.810 --> 00:28:19.966
if you authorize your users to
create a data set in BigQuery,

00:28:19.966 --> 00:28:21.840
when they create that
data set, they actually

00:28:21.840 --> 00:28:25.170
become the owner on the data
set access control list.

00:28:25.170 --> 00:28:27.570
And the consequence of that
is they can turn around

00:28:27.570 --> 00:28:29.970
and share that data
set, even with users

00:28:29.970 --> 00:28:31.300
outside your organization.

00:28:31.300 --> 00:28:31.800
Right?

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:33.540
So for us, this is
like a huge shock

00:28:33.540 --> 00:28:36.540
because we're trying to
prevent data leakage,

00:28:36.540 --> 00:28:39.210
and obviously we can't
have sensitive data loaded

00:28:39.210 --> 00:28:42.960
into a data set and
then just any user

00:28:42.960 --> 00:28:44.920
can share that with the world.

00:28:44.920 --> 00:28:48.480
So an example of how we use
automation to fix that is we've

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:50.460
actually written a
server list cloud

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:54.210
function that creates data
sets on behalf of users.

00:28:54.210 --> 00:28:56.940
And so they call that cloud
function, the cloud function

00:28:56.940 --> 00:28:59.690
creates the data set, and it
strips all of the owners out

00:28:59.690 --> 00:29:01.654
of the ACL, kind of
fixes it up, and then

00:29:01.654 --> 00:29:04.320
it returns back to the user, you
know, here's your new data set.

00:29:04.320 --> 00:29:07.390
You can use it at this link.

00:29:07.390 --> 00:29:10.530
And really the last piece
is, even though we've

00:29:10.530 --> 00:29:12.750
taken an approach that
uses defense in depth,

00:29:12.750 --> 00:29:14.460
that uses automation,
that's really

00:29:14.460 --> 00:29:16.560
kind of the day one
setup of the environment

00:29:16.560 --> 00:29:18.040
and it drifts over time.

00:29:18.040 --> 00:29:21.180
So we use in conjunction
Cloud Security Command

00:29:21.180 --> 00:29:25.140
Center and Forseti really
to monitor our environment,

00:29:25.140 --> 00:29:28.560
to audit and ensure that those
security guard rails are still

00:29:28.560 --> 00:29:32.530
in place, and then to surface
any violations of that to us.

00:29:32.530 --> 00:29:33.990
So I'm just curious,
show of hands,

00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:37.380
how many people are
familiar with Forseti?

00:29:37.380 --> 00:29:37.950
Awesome.

00:29:37.950 --> 00:29:38.610
Cool.

00:29:38.610 --> 00:29:40.700
OK, so Two Sigma is a
contributor to Forseti,

00:29:40.700 --> 00:29:43.360
and I encourage you guys
to become contributors

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:46.380
if you're interested, but
working with the Cloud Security

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:49.600
Command Center and Forseti teams
has been extremely positive.

00:29:49.600 --> 00:29:52.200
They've been super
helpful and we've

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:54.754
been able to contribute
some codes to Forseti

00:29:54.754 --> 00:29:56.670
and to get that merged
into the latest branch,

00:29:56.670 --> 00:29:58.570
so I'll talk a little
bit about that later.

00:29:58.570 --> 00:30:01.240
But for those of you who
haven't used Forseti or heard

00:30:01.240 --> 00:30:03.510
or Forseti, it's
really a tool that

00:30:03.510 --> 00:30:06.690
lets you scan your
environment and you

00:30:06.690 --> 00:30:10.730
can write very simple
rule sets in YAML format.

00:30:10.730 --> 00:30:14.320
As an example, if we keep
using this BigQuery example,

00:30:14.320 --> 00:30:17.610
you could write a rule that says
I don't want any @twosigma.com

00:30:17.610 --> 00:30:21.820
users in my data set ACL ever.

00:30:21.820 --> 00:30:23.740
And Forseti will
run periodically,

00:30:23.740 --> 00:30:25.950
it will scan through
all of your data sets,

00:30:25.950 --> 00:30:28.830
and it will find
violations of that rule.

00:30:28.830 --> 00:30:30.720
And you can surface
those in various ways.

00:30:30.720 --> 00:30:32.905
You can get an
email to yourself.

00:30:32.905 --> 00:30:34.530
I know they're building
out integration

00:30:34.530 --> 00:30:36.437
with Pub/Sub and Slack.

00:30:36.437 --> 00:30:38.520
But of course we use Cloud
Security Command Center

00:30:38.520 --> 00:30:40.970
as kind of our
central pane of glass,

00:30:40.970 --> 00:30:42.970
and so we wanted to surface
commands into there,

00:30:42.970 --> 00:30:44.595
and that's possible
today with Forseti.

00:30:44.595 --> 00:30:47.430
There is integration
between the two.

00:30:47.430 --> 00:30:52.380
So let me walk you through
a little bit of an example.

00:30:52.380 --> 00:30:54.690
This is kind of what
I'm going to demo,

00:30:54.690 --> 00:30:57.000
but I just wanted to show
you guys what happens first.

00:30:57.000 --> 00:30:57.500
Right?

00:30:57.500 --> 00:31:00.144
So it's very similar to
the BigQuery example.

00:31:00.144 --> 00:31:01.560
So let's say you
have a user, Bob.

00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:04.560
He joins your company and
he wants to create a bucket.

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:07.560
So he's going install the
Google Command Line Utility

00:31:07.560 --> 00:31:12.660
and he's going to make a simple
API call to create that bucket.

00:31:12.660 --> 00:31:15.780
And the unintended consequence
of this is in the back-end.

00:31:15.780 --> 00:31:18.240
The data set has an
access control list,

00:31:18.240 --> 00:31:20.880
and Bob just became
owner on that bucket

00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:22.710
and he can now share it, right?

00:31:22.710 --> 00:31:25.620
And if you're trying to
protect buckets and make sure

00:31:25.620 --> 00:31:28.055
that they don't get shared
outside of your organization,

00:31:28.055 --> 00:31:30.690
this is kind of an
undesirable effect, right?

00:31:30.690 --> 00:31:33.900
So bucket owner equals Bob.

00:31:33.900 --> 00:31:34.620
Owners can share.

00:31:34.620 --> 00:31:36.240
I kind of already
explained that.

00:31:36.240 --> 00:31:39.160
This opens up a very easy
path for data leakage.

00:31:39.160 --> 00:31:40.095
So what we would do--

00:31:40.095 --> 00:31:42.240
I guess, if we were
thinking about things

00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:46.270
from a very manual perspective,
as a diligent security team,

00:31:46.270 --> 00:31:49.410
we would want to go modify
the ACL on that bucket, right?

00:31:49.410 --> 00:31:52.290
And of course there's like
an API call to do that,

00:31:52.290 --> 00:31:54.210
so we could install the
Command Line Utility

00:31:54.210 --> 00:31:56.910
and say I want to change
the ACL on the bucket

00:31:56.910 --> 00:32:00.600
and I want to set Bob to be
an editor and not an owner.

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:03.600
But you can imagine that
as your organization scales

00:32:03.600 --> 00:32:06.720
and there's hundreds or
thousands or tens of thousands

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:08.940
of users all creating
buckets, you're

00:32:08.940 --> 00:32:11.700
not going to hire 100
people on your security team

00:32:11.700 --> 00:32:14.730
to constantly go change the ACL
on all of the buckets, right?

00:32:14.730 --> 00:32:16.710
So what you would probably
do in that scenario

00:32:16.710 --> 00:32:19.110
is you would abstract it
away with a cloud function

00:32:19.110 --> 00:32:22.230
or something like that,
like I explained before,

00:32:22.230 --> 00:32:25.020
but that's kind of
only the initial setup.

00:32:25.020 --> 00:32:27.300
That means that when
the bucket gets created,

00:32:27.300 --> 00:32:30.086
you're going to remove
unintended owners from the ACL.

00:32:30.086 --> 00:32:32.460
It doesn't mean that it's
going to stay that way forever,

00:32:32.460 --> 00:32:32.820
right?

00:32:32.820 --> 00:32:34.361
And there's always
going to be, like,

00:32:34.361 --> 00:32:36.120
super users in your
organization that

00:32:36.120 --> 00:32:41.590
can go and make changes, whether
maliciously or just by mistake.

00:32:41.590 --> 00:32:45.790
So this is where Cloud Security
Command Center really helps us.

00:32:45.790 --> 00:32:48.000
Andy talked a little bit
about the security marks

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:48.880
that you can apply.

00:32:48.880 --> 00:32:51.570
So we use these in a
bit of a different way.

00:32:51.570 --> 00:32:55.140
We mark resources in
Security Command Center

00:32:55.140 --> 00:32:56.870
with a security level, right?

00:32:56.870 --> 00:33:00.430
So it could be like open,
internal, restricted, super,

00:33:00.430 --> 00:33:02.190
super restricted.

00:33:02.190 --> 00:33:04.590
And what we do is we use
these marks to differentiate

00:33:04.590 --> 00:33:07.770
resources and we
can apply scanning

00:33:07.770 --> 00:33:11.304
to only certain classes of
resources that we care about.

00:33:11.304 --> 00:33:13.220
So let's, like, just
walk through that, right?

00:33:13.220 --> 00:33:16.440
What we would do, rather
than creating buckets

00:33:16.440 --> 00:33:19.190
on behalf of the users, is
we would create that security

00:33:19.190 --> 00:33:21.705
mark, and in this case,
we're just saying the buckets

00:33:21.705 --> 00:33:23.580
that we care about are
restricted-- they have

00:33:23.580 --> 00:33:26.060
a security level of restricted.

00:33:26.060 --> 00:33:28.410
And now let's say that
someone makes a mistake

00:33:28.410 --> 00:33:33.070
and they add Alice as an
owner on that bucket ACL.

00:33:33.070 --> 00:33:35.730
We have some very simple code
that we run on a periodic

00:33:35.730 --> 00:33:38.100
basis-- and you could run
this in a Cloud Function,

00:33:38.100 --> 00:33:39.570
you could run this
in App Engine,

00:33:39.570 --> 00:33:42.028
you could just run this on a
VM if you're not familiar with

00:33:42.028 --> 00:33:43.110
those services--

00:33:43.110 --> 00:33:46.321
and what that code does
is it will go reach out

00:33:46.321 --> 00:33:48.570
to Cloud Security Command
Center and will say, give me

00:33:48.570 --> 00:33:51.300
a list of all of my
resources, all of my assets,

00:33:51.300 --> 00:33:54.150
that are security
level restricted.

00:33:54.150 --> 00:33:56.790
And if any of them have
kind of unintended changes,

00:33:56.790 --> 00:33:59.490
like say the bucket ACL has been
changed in the past 10 minutes

00:33:59.490 --> 00:34:02.355
or 24 hours, create a finding.

00:34:02.355 --> 00:34:05.695
Create a finding on that asset
and Security Command Center

00:34:05.695 --> 00:34:08.070
so that the incident response
team can go and take a look

00:34:08.070 --> 00:34:11.270
at that and kind of fix it.

00:34:11.270 --> 00:34:12.830
So I'll show you
a simple demo now.

00:34:12.830 --> 00:34:15.242
We'll switch over to the demo.

00:34:15.242 --> 00:34:16.190
Awesome.

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:22.659
So I've got this bucket
here called Next 18 Demo,

00:34:22.659 --> 00:34:24.536
and you can see on the--

00:34:24.536 --> 00:34:26.286
can everyone see that
or should I zoom in?

00:34:26.286 --> 00:34:28.840
I'll zoom in a little bit.

00:34:28.840 --> 00:34:31.449
Right, so it's got a
security level of Restricted.

00:34:31.449 --> 00:34:34.560
If I just dive into
it, you'll see here,

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:36.639
it's marked as restricted.

00:34:36.639 --> 00:34:39.659
And right now,
there's 14 findings.

00:34:39.659 --> 00:34:42.820
Now I want to point out, we
hope to see more findings

00:34:42.820 --> 00:34:44.350
if the demo is successful.

00:34:44.350 --> 00:34:46.525
Those 14 are for me testing
it so that I could make

00:34:46.525 --> 00:34:47.650
sure it worked for you all.

00:34:50.739 --> 00:34:52.600
Right, so we've got that bucket.

00:34:52.600 --> 00:34:54.850
It's got 14 findings right now.

00:34:54.850 --> 00:34:57.400
And the thing we care
about is whether or not

00:34:57.400 --> 00:35:01.151
the ACL has changed in whatever
time period we've defined.

00:35:01.151 --> 00:35:02.650
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:35:02.650 --> 00:35:05.840
to go over to the actual bucket
here, and in the console,

00:35:05.840 --> 00:35:08.360
you can actually edit the
permissions of the bucket.

00:35:08.360 --> 00:35:10.840
And this is how the access
control list on the bucket

00:35:10.840 --> 00:35:12.350
gets controlled.

00:35:12.350 --> 00:35:21.107
So what I'm going to do is
I'm going to add myself,

00:35:21.107 --> 00:35:22.940
and I'm just going to
pick something random,

00:35:22.940 --> 00:35:27.410
like, say, storage
legacy bucket owner.

00:35:27.410 --> 00:35:29.690
Awesome.

00:35:29.690 --> 00:35:33.460
So you can see I've added
myself to the bucket ACL,

00:35:33.460 --> 00:35:35.289
so the ACL should
have changed now.

00:35:35.289 --> 00:35:36.830
And what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:35:36.830 --> 00:35:41.610
to just rescan all of our
assets in Cloud Security Command

00:35:41.610 --> 00:35:42.110
Center.

00:35:42.110 --> 00:35:44.030
This should take
about a minute or two.

00:35:44.030 --> 00:35:48.370
So while this is
running, I'm just

00:35:48.370 --> 00:35:50.620
going to walk you guys through
the code-- a little bit

00:35:50.620 --> 00:35:51.119
of the code.

00:35:54.850 --> 00:35:57.190
And today we're running this
on, like, an hourly basis,

00:35:57.190 --> 00:36:00.490
but we could speed this
up if we wanted to.

00:36:00.490 --> 00:36:02.170
And I guess it's
worth pointing out

00:36:02.170 --> 00:36:05.080
that initially, Cloud
Security Command Center only

00:36:05.080 --> 00:36:07.780
had a REST API exposed but
now there is a Python Client

00:36:07.780 --> 00:36:11.410
Library, so for those of you
who are familiar with Python,

00:36:11.410 --> 00:36:14.290
it's very simple to interact
with Security Command Center

00:36:14.290 --> 00:36:18.940
and write some simple code that
interacts with it, basically.

00:36:18.940 --> 00:36:22.060
Right, so I've got this method
here called checkChanges,

00:36:22.060 --> 00:36:23.230
and this is a very simple.

00:36:23.230 --> 00:36:24.646
All it's going to
do is it's going

00:36:24.646 --> 00:36:26.800
to take a start time
of 15 minutes ago

00:36:26.800 --> 00:36:28.930
and an end time of
now, and it's going

00:36:28.930 --> 00:36:32.920
to say, in that 15-minute
window, get all of the assets

00:36:32.920 --> 00:36:35.230
from Security Command
Center that have a security

00:36:35.230 --> 00:36:39.040
level of restricted mark
on them, and if any of them

00:36:39.040 --> 00:36:43.830
have changes that we care
about, create a finding, right?

00:36:43.830 --> 00:36:48.250
So there's another method called
findChanges and this makes

00:36:48.250 --> 00:36:51.700
a call out to Security Command
Center, get assets by mark.

00:36:51.700 --> 00:36:53.680
So it's one of the API
methods you can use.

00:36:53.680 --> 00:36:55.720
You can retrieve
all of the assets

00:36:55.720 --> 00:36:58.582
from Security Command Center
that have a certain mark.

00:36:58.582 --> 00:37:00.040
And of course for
different assets,

00:37:00.040 --> 00:37:02.165
you're going to care about
different things, right?

00:37:02.165 --> 00:37:05.140
For a bucket, we care about
the ACL and the owners

00:37:05.140 --> 00:37:07.810
on the bucket, but for something
like an instance or a firewall,

00:37:07.810 --> 00:37:09.770
you might care about
different properties.

00:37:09.770 --> 00:37:12.640
So we just have a simple if
statement here that says,

00:37:12.640 --> 00:37:16.480
for all of the assets that
come back, sort them by type,

00:37:16.480 --> 00:37:18.290
and if it's a
bucket-- in this case,

00:37:18.290 --> 00:37:20.570
it's the only one we're
checking in the demo--

00:37:20.570 --> 00:37:24.070
we go into this method
here, bucketChanges.

00:37:24.070 --> 00:37:26.890
And so what we then do
is we make another call

00:37:26.890 --> 00:37:29.560
to Cloud Security Command Center
and we get the final asset.

00:37:29.560 --> 00:37:31.750
So we have the
asset representation

00:37:31.750 --> 00:37:33.770
at the beginning of
the 15-minute window

00:37:33.770 --> 00:37:35.920
and then at the end of
the 15-minute window,

00:37:35.920 --> 00:37:37.720
and we compare those two.

00:37:37.720 --> 00:37:40.180
So here's the one we're
kind of interested in.

00:37:40.180 --> 00:37:43.390
We say, if the
initial asset's ACL

00:37:43.390 --> 00:37:45.840
is different than the
final asset's ACL,

00:37:45.840 --> 00:37:47.770
then just call out to
Security Command Center

00:37:47.770 --> 00:37:52.870
and create a finding that says
the bucket ACL has changed.

00:37:52.870 --> 00:37:56.140
And I'll show you how simple
it is to actually create

00:37:56.140 --> 00:37:57.800
that finding.

00:37:57.800 --> 00:38:02.350
So that was all, like, kind of
my custom logic code and stuff.

00:38:02.350 --> 00:38:04.810
This method here,
which is probably

00:38:04.810 --> 00:38:08.740
about five lines of code, is
creating a finding in Cloud

00:38:08.740 --> 00:38:09.740
Security Command Center.

00:38:09.740 --> 00:38:11.281
So that's really
all you need, right?

00:38:11.281 --> 00:38:13.870
We generate a unique
ID for the finding,

00:38:13.870 --> 00:38:16.060
we specify the
category, we specify

00:38:16.060 --> 00:38:20.577
the source, and an arbitrary
JSON blob of properties,

00:38:20.577 --> 00:38:23.160
and then just the asset that we
want to create the finding on.

00:38:23.160 --> 00:38:25.480
So in this case, it's a bucket.

00:38:25.480 --> 00:38:27.230
So I'll actually
run the demo now.

00:38:29.900 --> 00:38:31.634
We'll hope it works.

00:38:31.634 --> 00:38:33.300
So it scaned through
a bunch of buckets,

00:38:33.300 --> 00:38:37.790
and you can see there for the
Next 18 Demo bucket, which

00:38:37.790 --> 00:38:40.651
is the one that I just
