00:39:00.610 --> 00:39:01.940
And yeah, that's really it.

00:39:01.940 --> 00:39:04.010
So what we do is we
basically run this code--

00:39:04.010 --> 00:39:07.410
and we've extended it since then
to look at different things,

00:39:07.410 --> 00:39:09.540
not just buckets
and their ACLs--

00:39:09.540 --> 00:39:11.600
and we run this
just on a VM today

00:39:11.600 --> 00:39:16.190
and it wakes up every so often,
runs, surfaces findings to us,

00:39:16.190 --> 00:39:18.510
and then we react
to them, basically.

00:39:18.510 --> 00:39:21.640
So we'll flip back
to the slides.

00:39:21.640 --> 00:39:24.570
Awesome.

00:39:24.570 --> 00:39:26.820
So I wanted to talk just a
little bit before I get off

00:39:26.820 --> 00:39:29.790
stage about Cloud Security
Command Center and Forseti

00:39:29.790 --> 00:39:32.060
and the integration
between the two.

00:39:32.060 --> 00:39:34.990
So when we first got exposed
to these two services,

00:39:34.990 --> 00:39:37.360
the way it worked is,
Forseti would basically

00:39:37.360 --> 00:39:40.120
construct a list of
violations that it found based

00:39:40.120 --> 00:39:41.950
on all the rules
that we had written

00:39:41.950 --> 00:39:43.781
and it would push
these to a GCS bucket,

00:39:43.781 --> 00:39:46.030
and then Cloud Security
Command Center would reach out

00:39:46.030 --> 00:39:49.280
to that GCS bucket, it would
parse all of the violations,

00:39:49.280 --> 00:39:51.400
and it would load
them into findings.

00:39:51.400 --> 00:39:53.260
We thought this was
a little bit clunky.

00:39:53.260 --> 00:39:55.420
It was the only thing
available at the time.

00:39:55.420 --> 00:39:58.060
But then, one day Andy
and I were talking,

00:39:58.060 --> 00:40:00.400
and the API for Cloud
Security Command Center

00:40:00.400 --> 00:40:02.350
had been exposed,
and so we thought,

00:40:02.350 --> 00:40:04.909
why not make this a
little bit better?

00:40:04.909 --> 00:40:06.700
So what we did is we
worked kind of closely

00:40:06.700 --> 00:40:09.670
with the Forseti and the Cloud
Security Command Center teams

00:40:09.670 --> 00:40:12.250
and we modified that code
so that we use the Security

00:40:12.250 --> 00:40:14.210
Command Center API directly.

00:40:14.210 --> 00:40:16.540
So for any of you who
are using Forseti today,

00:40:16.540 --> 00:40:18.430
you'll be familiar
with a YAML file

00:40:18.430 --> 00:40:19.900
that you configure Forseti with.

00:40:19.900 --> 00:40:22.360
You tell it, like, what
notifiers you want to run,

00:40:22.360 --> 00:40:25.210
what rules you want
to run in the scanner.

00:40:25.210 --> 00:40:28.270
There's a new section in where
you enable the Cloud Security

00:40:28.270 --> 00:40:30.970
Command Center integration for.

00:40:30.970 --> 00:40:34.460
You can now say either use the
bucket mode or the API mode.

00:40:34.460 --> 00:40:36.520
And if you set it
to API mode, it's

00:40:36.520 --> 00:40:39.500
going to use the API directly
rather than using that GCS

00:40:39.500 --> 00:40:41.099
method of writing to a bucket.

00:40:41.099 --> 00:40:42.640
And there was really
a couple reasons

00:40:42.640 --> 00:40:43.681
why we wanted to do this.

00:40:43.681 --> 00:40:45.240
One was obviously
it's just cleaner

00:40:45.240 --> 00:40:46.990
to use the API
directly and not have

00:40:46.990 --> 00:40:49.840
to rely on that latency of
writing something to GCS

00:40:49.840 --> 00:40:52.240
and then waiting
for it to be read,

00:40:52.240 --> 00:40:55.120
but the other reason is we
have a lot more control now

00:40:55.120 --> 00:40:58.235
within the Forseti code base to
publish whatever properties we

00:40:58.235 --> 00:41:00.610
want directly into that finding
in Cloud Security Command

00:41:00.610 --> 00:41:01.520
Center.

00:41:01.520 --> 00:41:04.450
So based on the rules that
your scanner is using,

00:41:04.450 --> 00:41:07.270
you can write different property
fields into Cloud Security

00:41:07.270 --> 00:41:08.470
Command Center.

00:41:08.470 --> 00:41:10.780
All right, so that's it for me.

