00:58:57.675 --> 00:59:00.090
so, and customers as well--

00:59:00.090 --> 00:59:03.040
providing APIs to
access the data

00:59:03.040 --> 00:59:05.330
that is present in the
application framework.

00:59:05.330 --> 00:59:08.320
So as a customer,
if you consent so,

00:59:08.320 --> 00:59:10.390
you can give access to
your security data that

00:59:10.390 --> 00:59:12.610
was gathered from
Palo Alto Networks

00:59:12.610 --> 00:59:14.740
and is stored in the
Palo Alto Networks cloud

00:59:14.740 --> 00:59:16.300
to a third-party solution.

00:59:16.300 --> 00:59:18.250
And through APIs, these
third-party solutions

00:59:18.250 --> 00:59:22.870
can integrate and do analytics,
do integration, do treat intel.

00:59:22.870 --> 00:59:24.450
There are lots of use cases.

00:59:24.450 --> 00:59:29.440
And again, vendors and customers
will be able to do this.

00:59:29.440 --> 00:59:31.514
And why we think
this is important.

00:59:31.514 --> 00:59:33.430
We think that this is
important because if you

00:59:33.430 --> 00:59:36.130
look at the security
consumption model,

00:59:36.130 --> 00:59:38.200
how many security
vendors are out there?

00:59:38.200 --> 00:59:39.040
A lot.

00:59:39.040 --> 00:59:42.790
And each single security vendor
provides some unique value

00:59:42.790 --> 00:59:44.650
to customers.

00:59:44.650 --> 00:59:46.720
The problem is that
if you want to try

00:59:46.720 --> 00:59:48.700
one of those vendors,
one of those solutions,

00:59:48.700 --> 00:59:51.677
or if you just want to build
a solution because you are

00:59:51.677 --> 00:59:54.260
a vendor and you want to build
a solution that does analytics,

00:59:54.260 --> 00:59:59.470
for example, you will need to
deploy sensors, deploy agents

00:59:59.470 --> 01:00:01.930
as a customer, and
as a vendor, you

01:00:01.930 --> 01:00:03.827
need to write
sensors, write agents,

01:00:03.827 --> 01:00:05.910
but your core business is
really analytics, maybe,

01:00:05.910 --> 01:00:06.642
or threat intel.

01:00:06.642 --> 01:00:08.350
You don't really need
to build that stuff

01:00:08.350 --> 01:00:10.420
but you need that in
order for your customers

01:00:10.420 --> 01:00:13.960
to consume your
unique value, right?

01:00:13.960 --> 01:00:17.200
And for customers, it's
painful to do these POCs.

01:00:17.200 --> 01:00:19.630
They are long and complex.

01:00:19.630 --> 01:00:21.280
The other problem
is that if you have

01:00:21.280 --> 01:00:24.280
lots of security solutions, you
have to deploy lots of things.

01:00:24.280 --> 01:00:28.840
Infrastructure costs, operations
costs, is hard to manage.

01:00:28.840 --> 01:00:32.350
And lastly, integrating all of
these different data sources

01:00:32.350 --> 01:00:34.960
and providing an effective
automation workflow that

01:00:34.960 --> 01:00:38.020
protects the customer is hard.

01:00:38.020 --> 01:00:40.210
So with something like
application framework,

01:00:40.210 --> 01:00:43.000
we want to be the
sensors and enforcer

01:00:43.000 --> 01:00:45.220
in the network in the
endpoint in the cloud

01:00:45.220 --> 01:00:49.480
and allow, again, third parties
and customers to leverage

01:00:49.480 --> 01:00:52.450
our data to do analytics.

01:00:52.450 --> 01:00:54.760
So if you want to try
a third-party solution,

01:00:54.760 --> 01:00:57.871
you can just grant them access
to your data as a customer

01:00:57.871 --> 01:00:58.870
and they're ready to go.

01:00:58.870 --> 01:01:00.880
If you're a vendor and you
want to build a solution that

01:01:00.880 --> 01:01:02.470
integrates with
that framework, you

01:01:02.470 --> 01:01:05.270
don't need to deploy and
create sensors and agents.

01:01:05.270 --> 01:01:07.870
You just call our APIs.

01:01:07.870 --> 01:01:09.370
So these are very
effective and you

01:01:09.370 --> 01:01:11.920
don't need to reinvest or
reinvent the wheel every time.

01:01:11.920 --> 01:01:14.050
And the automation
capabilities that we can bring

01:01:14.050 --> 01:01:15.642
there are very interesting.

01:01:15.642 --> 01:01:17.350
In fact, when we
started working on this,

01:01:17.350 --> 01:01:19.444
we were thinking
about some use cases,

01:01:19.444 --> 01:01:21.360
and the more we worked
with different partners

01:01:21.360 --> 01:01:24.150
and different customers, we
learned additional use cases

01:01:24.150 --> 01:01:25.990
that we didn't even think about.

01:01:25.990 --> 01:01:28.357
So that's really exciting.

01:01:28.357 --> 01:01:29.940
So this is what we're
doing right now.

01:01:29.940 --> 01:01:35.770
And back to the Security Command
Center integration point--

01:01:35.770 --> 01:01:37.653
no, sorry, back to the slides.

01:01:42.950 --> 01:01:44.040
OK.

01:01:44.040 --> 01:01:47.340
So what we did is
building an integration

01:01:47.340 --> 01:01:49.950
between the application
framework and the Security

01:01:49.950 --> 01:01:51.000
Command Center.

01:01:51.000 --> 01:01:52.450
It's a simple application.

01:01:52.450 --> 01:01:54.870
We build it using
serverless components.

01:01:54.870 --> 01:01:56.350
We used the Python SDK as well.

01:01:56.350 --> 01:01:58.110
That was pretty easy to do.

01:01:58.110 --> 01:02:00.210
And the idea is that
the data is coming

01:02:00.210 --> 01:02:01.920
from Traps, our
endpoint solution,

01:02:01.920 --> 01:02:03.780
from the firewalls
running in GCP.

01:02:03.780 --> 01:02:06.480
All this data is flowing
in application framework.

01:02:06.480 --> 01:02:08.760
And from there, the
Security Command Center app

01:02:08.760 --> 01:02:11.910
is able to pick the data
up and generate findings

01:02:11.910 --> 01:02:13.830
into Security Command Center.

01:02:13.830 --> 01:02:15.420
And through some
user interface, you

01:02:15.420 --> 01:02:17.700
can actually enrich those
findings with PI data.

01:02:17.700 --> 01:02:20.550
You can act upon
that data, providing

01:02:20.550 --> 01:02:22.710
blocking and remediation
in the firewalls.

01:02:22.710 --> 01:02:24.870
Potentially enable
third-party integrations

01:02:24.870 --> 01:02:26.290
on top of that as well.

01:02:26.290 --> 01:02:30.040
So this will become
clear in a second.

01:02:30.040 --> 01:02:34.260
So what we are doing right
now is showing this flow.

01:02:34.260 --> 01:02:36.984
So, on the bottom,
you have an endpoint,

01:02:36.984 --> 01:02:38.400
which actually is
the machine when

01:02:38.400 --> 01:02:42.900
I try to bitcoin mine,
that had a malware that

01:02:42.900 --> 01:02:46.230
was blocked by our
endpoint protection traps.

01:02:46.230 --> 01:02:49.680
And the logs are being
sent from the endpoint

01:02:49.680 --> 01:02:51.940
to application framework.

01:02:51.940 --> 01:02:55.760
This will actually
be stored there

01:02:55.760 --> 01:02:57.720
and the Security
Command Center app

01:02:57.720 --> 01:02:59.880
that we wrote would
pick up this data,

01:02:59.880 --> 01:03:02.780
detect that there is an
alert, and create a finding

01:03:02.780 --> 01:03:04.570
in Security Command Center.

01:03:04.570 --> 01:03:06.870
So we will see a finding
there, hopefully.

01:03:06.870 --> 01:03:11.250
And from there, we will open
another console that we wrote

01:03:11.250 --> 01:03:14.300
again for the purpose of this
integration-- very simple--

01:03:14.300 --> 01:03:16.020
where we can do
additional analytics,

01:03:16.020 --> 01:03:19.830
look at additional data,
and reach that alert

01:03:19.830 --> 01:03:22.650
with threat intel
information that we have

01:03:22.650 --> 01:03:26.190
and eventually block
the associated command

01:03:26.190 --> 01:03:29.550
and control IP addresses
on the firewalls.

01:03:29.550 --> 01:03:32.840
So protecting the other machines
that are running in the cloud.

01:03:32.840 --> 01:03:36.090
And if, for some reason, there
is some machine that actually

01:03:36.090 --> 01:03:37.620
has been infected
by that malware

01:03:37.620 --> 01:03:40.460
because maybe my colleague
wasn't that smart

01:03:40.460 --> 01:03:42.512
and he didn't
install traps on it,

01:03:42.512 --> 01:03:44.802
the firewall will now
block it and will tell us

01:03:44.802 --> 01:03:47.260
that something is going on,
and we'll see additional alerts

01:03:47.260 --> 01:03:49.300
in Security Command Center.

01:03:49.300 --> 01:03:52.817
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:03:52.817 --> 01:03:55.150
FRANCESCO VIGO: You can do--
so the question is, can you

01:03:55.150 --> 01:03:57.770
do the enrichment before
creating the alert?

01:03:57.770 --> 01:03:58.600
Yes, you could.

01:03:58.600 --> 01:04:02.830
So the idea is, we are doing
that manually, so as a security

01:04:02.830 --> 01:04:06.610
analyst, I decide that I want to
enrich the data because I think

01:04:06.610 --> 01:04:09.700
that this is an important
alert that I want to enrich,

01:04:09.700 --> 01:04:11.590
but obviously you could
change the workflow

01:04:11.590 --> 01:04:14.680
and automate that and add
threat intel before creating

01:04:14.680 --> 01:04:15.920
the alert.

01:04:15.920 --> 01:04:18.400
So let me go through the demo.

01:04:21.710 --> 01:04:22.990
OK, thank you.

01:04:22.990 --> 01:04:25.525
So this is the dashboard.

01:04:25.525 --> 01:04:26.400
There are two alerts.

01:04:26.400 --> 01:04:30.380
Now I refresh and
I see three alerts,

01:04:30.380 --> 01:04:31.880
which means that
it's working, which

01:04:31.880 --> 01:04:35.840
is wonderful because this is
an alpha product from Google.

01:04:35.840 --> 01:04:38.750
It's a staging environment
alpha for us as well

01:04:38.750 --> 01:04:41.570
and our DevOps teams did
some deployment today.

01:04:41.570 --> 01:04:43.870
So I'm lucky.

01:04:46.520 --> 01:04:49.830
So if I look at the
details of the finding,

01:04:49.830 --> 01:04:53.090
you will see that we
have some stuff in here,

01:04:53.090 --> 01:04:56.000
and one is called
Traps WildFire Malware.

01:04:56.000 --> 01:04:58.940
So this specific
finding was generated--

01:04:58.940 --> 01:05:01.700
you see on top-- eight minutes
ago and it actually tried

01:05:01.700 --> 01:05:03.796
to block--

01:05:03.796 --> 01:05:06.470
I mean, I tried to
run the bitcoin miner

01:05:06.470 --> 01:05:08.630
and that was blocked by Traps.

01:05:08.630 --> 01:05:10.790
And I have lots of
information here--

01:05:10.790 --> 01:05:12.657
the user that did
this, Francesco.

01:05:12.657 --> 01:05:14.740
The IP address of the
machine that generated that.

01:05:14.740 --> 01:05:16.400
The malware hash.

01:05:16.400 --> 01:05:17.930
And something
interesting also is

01:05:17.930 --> 01:05:21.560
that this is linked to
an asset in Google Cloud.

01:05:21.560 --> 01:05:24.590
And this is important because
Traps doesn't know anything

01:05:24.590 --> 01:05:27.350
about the fact that that's
an asset from Google Cloud.

01:05:27.350 --> 01:05:29.360
It just knows the IP address.

01:05:29.360 --> 01:05:32.210
And we used actually the
API provided by the Security

01:05:32.210 --> 01:05:35.300
Command Center to ask,
in our organization,

01:05:35.300 --> 01:05:38.720
which machine had this IP
address at this point in time

01:05:38.720 --> 01:05:40.190
when this event happened?

01:05:40.190 --> 01:05:43.110
And their API said winsrv1.

01:05:43.110 --> 01:05:44.910
Good, so we add it here.

01:05:44.910 --> 01:05:47.450
So this is the
event as we see it.

01:05:47.450 --> 01:05:49.460
And now I can open
the partner console,

01:05:49.460 --> 01:05:53.090
which is again a simple
application that we wrote.

01:05:53.090 --> 01:05:55.820
Right now I'm pulling
additional traffic and threat

01:05:55.820 --> 01:05:59.180
logs related to that machine
from the application framework.

01:05:59.180 --> 01:06:01.220
So I have firewall
logs and other stuff

01:06:01.220 --> 01:06:03.542
that I can pull
and visualize here.

01:06:03.542 --> 01:06:05.750
And again, I don't want to
get into too many details,

01:06:05.750 --> 01:06:08.237
but one of the things
that it can do is--

01:06:08.237 --> 01:06:09.320
this is suspicious, right?

01:06:09.320 --> 01:06:13.190
So let's see if Palo Alto
Networks knows some threat

01:06:13.190 --> 01:06:16.140
intel about this, a
specific piece of malware.

01:06:16.140 --> 01:06:18.410
So when I click on
Auto-Focus, I'm calling again

01:06:18.410 --> 01:06:23.000
our Cloud APIs and I'm pulling
threat intel information

01:06:23.000 --> 01:06:24.260
from our cloud.

01:06:24.260 --> 01:06:26.870
And if you see these tags--

01:06:26.870 --> 01:06:30.140
well, I'm not getting into the
details of which one of that

01:06:30.140 --> 01:06:32.330
means, but basically,
it's telling me

01:06:32.330 --> 01:06:34.550
that this is a specific
piece of malware that

01:06:34.550 --> 01:06:36.800
is part of a specific
campaign and there

01:06:36.800 --> 01:06:39.200
are some IP addresses and
a few [INAUDIBLE] that

01:06:39.200 --> 01:06:44.990
are associated to this
specific piece of malware.

01:06:44.990 --> 01:06:47.660
I would probably
want to block this.

01:06:47.660 --> 01:06:51.020
Before I do that, let me switch
back to the Security Command

01:06:51.020 --> 01:06:53.390
Center Console.

01:06:53.390 --> 01:06:59.090
So by doing that, we actually
added those Auto-Focus tags

01:06:59.090 --> 01:07:01.224
marks in the security event.

01:07:01.224 --> 01:07:03.140
So this means that even
if I don't have access

01:07:03.140 --> 01:07:04.056
to the other console--

01:07:04.056 --> 01:07:06.290
I just have access to
Security Command Center--

01:07:06.290 --> 01:07:09.530
the fact that myself as an
analyst did that enrichment,

01:07:09.530 --> 01:07:11.420
this information
becomes available also

01:07:11.420 --> 01:07:15.080
to my colleagues when they
look at this specific finding.

01:07:15.080 --> 01:07:18.590
So back to our console.

01:07:18.590 --> 01:07:19.570
I want to block this.

01:07:19.570 --> 01:07:22.620
I want to block the associated
command and control IP address.

01:07:22.620 --> 01:07:25.040
So what I'm going to do
is just click on Block,

01:07:25.040 --> 01:07:29.200
but before doing that, let me
switch over to the firewall.

01:07:29.200 --> 01:07:33.060
Again, I don't want to get into
the details on how this works,

01:07:33.060 --> 01:07:36.740
but this is our
firewall running in GCP.

01:07:36.740 --> 01:07:39.710
I created the policy that will
block all the IP addresses that

01:07:39.710 --> 01:07:44.390
are part of a specific
Dynamic Address Group, or DAG,

01:07:44.390 --> 01:07:47.270
and right now, this
group is empty.

01:07:47.270 --> 01:07:49.260
So there is nothing there.

01:07:49.260 --> 01:07:54.500
If I go back to my UI
and click on Block,

01:07:54.500 --> 01:07:55.880
through application
framework, we

01:07:55.880 --> 01:07:59.150
are telling the firewall,
OK, from now on, you

01:07:59.150 --> 01:08:01.760
should block the command
and control IP address

01:08:01.760 --> 01:08:03.560
associated to this malware.

01:08:03.560 --> 01:08:04.970
So if I'm lucky--

01:08:04.970 --> 01:08:08.960
and get back here, OK.

01:08:08.960 --> 01:08:12.110
You see in the firewall, that
IP address is now being blocked.

01:08:12.110 --> 01:08:14.210
And this information
again is also

01:08:14.210 --> 01:08:20.529
reflected in here--
in Security Command

01:08:20.529 --> 01:08:22.510
Center-- as an additional mark.

01:08:22.510 --> 01:08:25.060
So again, as a fellow
analyst, I can look into this

01:08:25.060 --> 01:08:27.122
and say, OK, someone
took care of this.

01:08:27.122 --> 01:08:28.430
This should be good enough.

01:08:28.430 --> 01:08:34.390
So I can actually do our solve
here, saying, OK, this incident

01:08:34.390 --> 01:08:35.470
has been taken care of.

01:08:35.470 --> 01:08:38.350
So I'll just click Resolve.

01:08:38.350 --> 01:08:42.729
And if I go back to
Security Command Center,

01:08:42.729 --> 01:08:44.390
this will now disappear.

01:08:44.390 --> 01:08:44.890
Why?

01:08:44.890 --> 01:08:47.979
Because this would
be marked as Resolved

01:08:47.979 --> 01:08:50.890
and I have a filter
here that is showing

01:08:50.890 --> 01:08:55.670
only the alerts and the
findings that are not resolved.

01:08:55.670 --> 01:08:57.760
So one last thing I
wanted to show you

01:08:57.760 --> 01:09:01.660
is that now that I actually
set the block on the firewall,

01:09:01.660 --> 01:09:06.340
I might want to see if there
is other activity related

01:09:06.340 --> 01:09:10.390
to that specific command and
control for that malware.

01:09:10.390 --> 01:09:14.309
And actually there
is, so my colleague

01:09:14.309 --> 01:09:15.850
has a machine that
is actually trying

01:09:15.850 --> 01:09:17.830
to connect to that
command and control

01:09:17.830 --> 01:09:19.810
and that was blocked now.

01:09:19.810 --> 01:09:23.620
And this is again something
that I can see on the firewall

01:09:23.620 --> 01:09:26.700
but also through the integration
with application framework.

01:09:26.700 --> 01:09:28.600
This might take a few
seconds to show up,

01:09:28.600 --> 01:09:31.330
because again, the log is going
through application framework

01:09:31.330 --> 01:09:32.830
and the application
that we wrote

01:09:32.830 --> 01:09:35.950
is pulling the data from
application framework

01:09:35.950 --> 01:09:38.840
and generating the finding
in Security Command Center.

01:09:38.840 --> 01:09:45.250
So let's wait a few
seconds, if we have them.

01:09:51.250 --> 01:09:55.200
I mean, the last bit of the last
demo of the last presentation

01:09:55.200 --> 01:09:56.440
of the day.

01:09:56.440 --> 01:09:58.860
Obviously there is
some suspense, right?

01:10:01.590 --> 01:10:03.160
Here we go.

01:10:03.160 --> 01:10:05.520
So C2 traffic blocked.

01:10:05.520 --> 01:10:07.950
Again, this is another machine.

01:10:07.950 --> 01:10:11.100
This alert is still coming
from application framework

01:10:11.100 --> 01:10:13.470
but originally has been
created by the firewall,

01:10:13.470 --> 01:10:15.840
not by the endpoint agent,
but the user experience

01:10:15.840 --> 01:10:17.710
is exactly the same.

01:10:17.710 --> 01:10:20.289
And we have different user
and other information.

01:10:20.289 --> 01:10:22.080
So the previous incident
has been resolved,

01:10:22.080 --> 01:10:24.930
but I have another
incident I should work on.

01:10:24.930 --> 01:10:27.640
So that's all it
had for the demo.

01:10:27.640 --> 01:10:32.120
Let's go back to the slides
one more time, please.

01:10:32.120 --> 01:10:35.020
Thank you.

01:10:35.020 --> 01:10:36.500
So takeaways.

01:10:36.500 --> 01:10:38.590
First of all, it was fun
and easy to integrate

01:10:38.590 --> 01:10:40.600
with Security Command Center.

01:10:40.600 --> 01:10:41.760
We had a great--

01:10:41.760 --> 01:10:43.480
again, Python
libraries were easy,

01:10:43.480 --> 01:10:46.400
serverless was pretty
good to work with,

01:10:46.400 --> 01:10:49.330
and Andy was great
in supporting us.

01:10:49.330 --> 01:10:52.750
And the other takeaway is that,
with application framework,

01:10:52.750 --> 01:10:56.170
we encourage vendors,
competitors, customers

01:10:56.170 --> 01:10:59.290
to come to us, talk to us,
and build applications that

01:10:59.290 --> 01:11:02.080
can leverage the data
we provide in order

01:11:02.080 --> 01:11:05.120
to provide value to yourselves
or to your customers.

01:11:05.120 --> 01:11:08.290
Please reach out to us,
because again, you can easily

01:11:08.290 --> 01:11:11.170
get access through the
Palo Alto Networks data

01:11:11.170 --> 01:11:14.890
if your customers allow
you to do so, of course.

01:11:14.890 --> 01:11:17.260
And with this
specific integration,

01:11:17.260 --> 01:11:20.350
we were able to build a
simple single pane of glass

01:11:20.350 --> 01:11:22.430
to do Security Command Center.

01:11:22.430 --> 01:11:24.010
So that's all I've got.

01:11:24.010 --> 01:11:28.048
I'm inviting Andy back
on stage for the wrap-up.

01:11:28.048 --> 01:11:28.996
Thank you.

01:11:33.581 --> 01:11:34.830
ANDY CHANG: Thanks, Francesco.

01:11:34.830 --> 01:11:37.790
So thank you so much.

01:11:37.790 --> 01:11:39.860
First, I think first
give a round of applause

01:11:39.860 --> 01:11:42.350
to all the speakers
who did live demos.

01:11:42.350 --> 01:11:46.299
We were four for four, so a
little bit of bravery there.

01:11:46.299 --> 01:11:47.840
I think to quickly
wrap up, because I

01:11:47.840 --> 01:11:51.447
know we're towards the end of
the day, some key questions

01:11:51.447 --> 01:11:53.030
that Cloud Security
Command Center has

01:11:53.030 --> 01:11:55.520
helped answer for customers
are around the assets.

01:11:55.520 --> 01:11:57.980
The fundamental question
is, what do I have?

01:11:57.980 --> 01:11:58.940
What am I running?

01:11:58.940 --> 01:12:01.730
I think our experience has been
that with the shadow IT that

01:12:01.730 --> 01:12:03.965
develops around cloud,
we actually provide

01:12:03.965 --> 01:12:05.600
this visibility to customers.

01:12:05.600 --> 01:12:07.970
Customers have sometimes
been very surprised.

01:12:07.970 --> 01:12:12.410
The average amount our
customers are off was about 25%.

01:12:12.410 --> 01:12:14.330
Some customers were off
by a factor of three

01:12:14.330 --> 01:12:16.897
as to how many assets they
were actually running in cloud.

01:12:16.897 --> 01:12:18.980
So that core visibility
is one of the first things

01:12:18.980 --> 01:12:20.390
they gain from this.

01:12:20.390 --> 01:12:23.930
As we saw with Two Sigma,
Cloudflare, and Palo Alto,

01:12:23.930 --> 01:12:25.340
the ability to
integrate findings

01:12:25.340 --> 01:12:27.320
from a lot of different
places to get really

01:12:27.320 --> 01:12:29.390
at things that are going
wrong, potential risk

01:12:29.390 --> 01:12:30.764
and vulnerabilities
in the cloud,

01:12:30.764 --> 01:12:32.330
has another been a huge win.

01:12:32.330 --> 01:12:35.960
Now, in addition, with our own
detectors and Cloud Anomaly

01:12:35.960 --> 01:12:38.720
Detection, the security
scanning, and the DLP,

01:12:38.720 --> 01:12:41.420
it's given further
visibility to our customers

01:12:41.420 --> 01:12:44.150
as to what's going
on their systems

01:12:44.150 --> 01:12:45.770
around the finding pieces.

01:12:45.770 --> 01:12:47.837
And as you can
see from the slide

01:12:47.837 --> 01:12:50.420
and going back to the original
slide about what we're building

01:12:50.420 --> 01:12:53.000
Command Center to try
to help you detect,

01:12:53.000 --> 01:12:55.980
it's issues around your
inventory, around your config,

01:12:55.980 --> 01:12:59.150
around access, around data,
and around the underlying

01:12:59.150 --> 01:13:02.030
applications you're running.

01:13:02.030 --> 01:13:04.760
In the public alpha
release that we announced,

01:13:04.760 --> 01:13:07.260
there are really five kind of
key things that are core of it

01:13:07.260 --> 01:13:10.080
and we demoed most of
these things today.

01:13:10.080 --> 01:13:11.280
The asset inventory pieces.

01:13:11.280 --> 01:13:14.120
Today, we support five
of the Google services

01:13:14.120 --> 01:13:15.660
in the asset inventory.

01:13:15.660 --> 01:13:18.210
The analysis to pieces that
have been used to allow

01:13:18.210 --> 01:13:19.970
you to find specific things.

01:13:19.970 --> 01:13:21.580
The marking part.

01:13:21.580 --> 01:13:24.840
What we didn't demo today are
kind of the notification piece

01:13:24.840 --> 01:13:28.380
that's built in to how you can
use Command Center to generate

01:13:28.380 --> 01:13:30.720
a Pub/Sub through a
Sidecar application

01:13:30.720 --> 01:13:34.050
and also to directly trigger
actions through cloud functions

01:13:34.050 --> 01:13:35.300
through a Sidecar application.

01:13:35.300 --> 01:13:39.120
The create findings API that
each of our customers and two

01:13:39.120 --> 01:13:40.650
partners have integrated with.

01:13:40.650 --> 01:13:44.574
And really again going back to
that top goal of covering each

01:13:44.574 --> 01:13:45.990
of the five different
things we're

01:13:45.990 --> 01:13:48.370
looking for from a
security standpoint.

01:13:48.370 --> 01:13:52.780
The ecosystem is starting
and continuously growing,

01:13:52.780 --> 01:13:55.260
and we're going to add more
this quarter into the following

01:13:55.260 --> 01:13:58.680
quarters, and we're hoping
that you folks in the audience,

01:13:58.680 --> 01:14:01.220
whether you're a direct
customer who's writing findings

01:14:01.220 --> 01:14:04.090
and wants your own detectors or
have specific partners that you

01:14:04.090 --> 01:14:05.590
want to integrate,
come talk to us

01:14:05.590 --> 01:14:08.240
and we can help prioritize that.

01:14:08.240 --> 01:14:12.740
So to learn more, we had
a blog post back in March.

01:14:12.740 --> 01:14:16.220
All of our documentation
is online and public.

01:14:16.220 --> 01:14:20.210
That includes the underlying
documentation as well as

01:14:20.210 --> 01:14:21.590
the API guides.

01:14:21.590 --> 01:14:23.510
And so with that, I
want to thank you guys.

01:14:23.510 --> 01:14:25.500
I want to invite the
other speakers back up.

01:14:25.500 --> 01:14:28.867
And I think we have a few
minutes for questions,

01:14:28.867 --> 01:14:31.200
but I also recognize that
we're keeping you from drinks,

01:14:31.200 --> 01:14:33.470
so I'm certainly
cool if you guys just

01:14:33.470 --> 01:14:35.260
want to call it a day.

01:14:35.260 --> 01:14:36.560
But thank you guys.

01:14:36.560 --> 01:14:38.060
[APPLAUSE]

01:14:38.060 --> 01:14:41.410
[MUSIC PLAYING]

